# A block on its header's own line holds exactly one statement.
#
# The header's line may carry the whole block, but a line still carries
# one statement (see PYKING.md, "a statement starts a line of its own").
# So the second `print` below is not a second statement of the `if`'s
# block -- it is a sibling of the `if` itself, sharing its line, which
# is the rule that catches it. Without that, `if c: print(1) print(2)`
# would read as a block of two and the line's meaning would depend on
# where the reader stopped.
#
# This program is expected to be REFUSED. Its `.ast.out` holds the error
# message, not a program's output.

x = 3

if x > 2: print("big") print("and also this")
